{"body":{"from":[{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00020YA"}],"opens":"Within a store everything is finite, so holons are simply counted, 1, 2, 3, by order and locality of birth, a reference within the store only its number. Nothing needs to be unique until stores meet: a union gives each store a prefix, as a mount gives a directory its place, and a store born of another is named by its parent, which counts its children. Names are then relative to the union that reads them; randomness, a key or a hash is needed only for a name meant to mean the same in every union, and can wait until it is. The prefix says where a holon was born, never where it stands, which stays a route.","restsOn":["one writer per store, branch as store","how a union assigns its prefixes","the code brought to the store"],"stands":"noted","title":"Names counted in locality, unified where stores meet"},"id":"1M6YBY3G0001XF7","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000007"]}
{"body":{"from":[{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00020YA"}],"opens":"The store is append-only, so where bytes are written never changes, and an object's address is its place: segment, offset, length. A hash on every object names everything twice, beside the holon's own identity, and costs near half the store with the indexes it forces; it is let go. Segments are one per publish, not per commit, so files are as few as publishes. Each commit keeps one sha256, a fixed-length fingerprint of the bytes it added and of its parent's: a hash of hashes, so one stands for the whole history, any part provable by a path that grows by one step each time what lies beneath doubles. And the lexicon a whole that grows by its parts, not one body rewritten at every new word.","restsOn":["the pack format rewritten to segments and offsets","the code brought to the store"],"stands":"noted","title":"Addresses by position, one fingerprint per commit"},"id":"1M6YBY3G0001XF8","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000007"]}
